<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trim, County Meath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trim is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-boyne">River Boyne</a> in County Meath, Ireland. Hugh de Lacy made it the centre of his Lordship of Meath after 1172, and Trim Castle, begun by him and completed by his son Walter, is the largest Anglo-Norman castle in Ireland. The town later became the seat of a liberty and hosted several sittings of the medieval Irish parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trim-county-meath.jpg" alt="Trim, County Meath" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nigel Thompson · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trim_Castle_and_the_River_Boyne,_Co._Meath_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7779602.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trim lies 61 m above sea level on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-boyne">River Boyne</a> in County Meath. Its Irish name, Áth Truimm or Átha Troim, means the ford of the elder trees, and the crossing at Watergate Street is thought to be the original ford. The town first appears in the record in an account in the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> of the foundation of a church there in 432 by Saint Lommán, the town's patron saint. It was historically the county town of Meath until the title passed in 1898 to the larger neighbouring town of Navan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A monastery was founded early at Trim, within the petty kingdom of the Cenél Lóegairi, and tradition ascribes it to Saint Patrick and Lommán. The church was burned several times in the twelfth century. The English captured the town during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Norman invasion of Ireland</a> and built a castle, but a large Irish army under Ruadhrí Ua Conchobair, King of Ireland, recaptured the town and burned the castle. Trim and the surrounding lands were granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath">Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath</a>, and the town became one of the most important Hiberno-Norman settlements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>Later it was refounded as St. Mary's Abbey under Augustinian rule, and its abbey church sheltered Our Lady of Trim, a wooden statue reported to work miracles. The statue made Trim a major pilgrimage site from at least 1397. It was burned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> dissolved the abbey. The Norman-Irish parliament met in Trim in the 15th century, and sessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> were sometimes held there, as in 1542. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> proposed Trim as the site of a Protestant university, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Sir Francis Drake</a> argued for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> instead. In 1649, after the sacking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a>, the garrison fled and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s army occupied the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Trim Castle, built in the late twelfth century, is the largest Norman castle in Ireland. The Yellow Steeple, the bell tower of the 14th-century St. Mary's Abbey, is the tallest building in the town, and other remains include the Black Friary, a 13th-century Dominican friary, and St. John's Priory, a medieval hospital. A column to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a> was raised in 1817. The 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the town a population of 9,563. Trim won the Irish Tidy Towns Competition in 1972, 1984, 2014 and 2022.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trim, County Meath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>De Lacy built motte castles across the territory and began the great stone castle at <b>Trim</b>, which became the largest Norman castle in Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-meath">Lordship of Meath</a></li></ul></section>
